WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.360
If you’ve been a close follower of this channel&nbsp;
for a while now, or even of the SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.280
Archive as a whole, then there’s a pretty high&nbsp;
chance that you might have heard of SCP-096.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.080
Even if this just so happens to be the first&nbsp;
of our SCP videos that you’ve ever clicked on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.720
you still might have seen the Shy Guy itself&nbsp;
in various other corners of the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.960
Since the earliest days of the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;
SCP-096 has gradually become one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.720
iconic and recognizable creatures in the&nbsp;
Foundation’s entire menagerie of anomalous beings.&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.960
Despite standing taller than any ordinary person,&nbsp;
you might be forgiven for mistaking SCP-096 for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.080
human being if you were to see it at a distance.&nbsp;
But the creature is unusually proportioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.000
to say the least, still appearing almost human&nbsp;
in its shape, but possessing long, dangling arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.240
that almost reach the ground. SCP-096’s body&nbsp;
is thin and hairless, with opaque, clear white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.600
pupil-less eyes and a wide, gaping mouth - or&nbsp;
so we’ve heard, thanks to drawings provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.240
by someone who sadly isn’t with us anymore...
But you might be surprised to learn that SCP-096&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.480
doesn’t really do all that much. Mostly, the tall,&nbsp;
spindly humanoid spends its waking days mindlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.680
pacing around in its containment cell. Sounds&nbsp;
like most days in a college dorm during lockdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.080
right? That is, of course, until someone dares&nbsp;
to look at SCP-096’s face. It doesn’t have to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.120
directly either, it can be something as simple&nbsp;
as glancing at a photo, or watching a video of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:38.880
the creature. But the moment it is seen, the exact&nbsp;
second it’s perceived, the wiry monster will know.&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.600
Having its face viewed in any way will cause&nbsp;
the Shy Guy to really live up to its nickname;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.560
the creature suffers some sort of psychological&nbsp;
meltdown, covering its face with its huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.840
claw-like hands and weeping. SCP-096&nbsp;
quickly becomes utterly inconsolable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.880
suffering from extreme emotional distress&nbsp;
until it simply can’t take it anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.080
Pulling itself up to its full, looming&nbsp;
height, SCP-096 will stand and travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.160
at impossible speeds towards the&nbsp;
person that dared look at its face.&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.200
There’s no way to hide from the Shy Guy,&nbsp;
nothing can stand in its path as it tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.280
down this unlucky person. And when it finds&nbsp;
them? Well, it’s certainly not pleasant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:25.280
Somehow, without leaving any trace on their body,&nbsp;
SCP-096 will kill the person that looked at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.360
possibly even eating them afterwards. Then, it&nbsp;
calms itself down, returns to its more docile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.560
state, and is typically bagged and brought back&nbsp;
home by whatever Mobile Task Force is closest.&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:42.560
SCP-096 has killed many of the teams sent to&nbsp;
contain it, proving itself to be resistant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.760
gunfire and other forms of conventional weaponry.&nbsp;
As much as being only a little as a tiny speck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.640
in a photograph is enough to trigger the&nbsp;
creature’s relentless defense mechanism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.920
Nobody can get away from it either, there is&nbsp;
literally nowhere in the entire world that someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.720
can run to escape SCP-096. Not even crossing&nbsp;
the oceans or jumping aboard a plane is enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.360
to stop it. And if our community questions&nbsp;
video on the matter is anything to go by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.880
you may not even be safe in outer space.
The SCP Foundation’s only real way to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.080
the creature safely under wraps and contained is&nbsp;
to lock it up and force all members of personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.760
to never, ever look at it, while they hunt down&nbsp;
any pieces of recorded media still out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.000
waiting like a ticking time bomb.
So, if no member of the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.720
can look at SCP-096, how do they interact with&nbsp;
it? Well, luckily, there’s a tale that answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:43.520
exactly that, known as “A Lesson in Power.”
Doctor Danielle Kowalski, a member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.200
Foundation’s research team assigned to SCP-096,&nbsp;
was sent into the creature’s cell wearing a sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.680
mask. You know, the kind you get on expensive&nbsp;
flights? What she was really wishing for though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.840
was a gas mask to go with it; Nobody&nbsp;
had warned her how bad SCP-096 smelled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.640
Then again though, few had been as close to the&nbsp;
creature as she had and lived to tell the tale.&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.120
As a child, Danielle Kowalski had a fear of&nbsp;
stepping into the ocean. Her mother used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.840
warn her about treading on a stingray and&nbsp;
dying as a result, giving her a phobia that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.360
stayed with her for many years. But Danielle’s&nbsp;
father had encouraged her to face her fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:24.080
to shuffle her feet on the ocean floor, kicking&nbsp;
up the sand so that the stingrays would know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.520
she was coming, and would simply just swim away.
Perhaps if he could have seen what kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.600
horrors Danielle would be working with at the&nbsp;
Foundation, her father wouldn’t have tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.040
make her so fearless. Maybe there are some&nbsp;
things that it’s logical to be afraid of.&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.240
Entering SCP-096’s cell, Doctor Kowalski&nbsp;
could hear the monster’s heaving breaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.760
the perfect mix between a frightened whimper and&nbsp;
a death rattle, the haunting sound of a person’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.240
final gasp of air. In the back of her mind,&nbsp;
Danielle hoped she didn’t touch the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.440
by accident. The closer she got, the tension and&nbsp;
the heat of its warm breath against her skin was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.200
making the approach all the more terrifying. She&nbsp;
had been issued with an instant camera, and took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:11.120
a photo in the dark. Click. The flash fired,&nbsp;
but with her eyes covered she couldn’t tell.&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.320
That was good though, that was the point.&nbsp;
If Danielle could see the flash going off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.200
she could see what else was in the room,&nbsp;
and that would put her in even more danger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:27.440
She was locked in with a kind of stingray that&nbsp;
wouldn’t swim away, even if it knew she was there.&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.600
Slowly, Doctor Kowalski began blindly&nbsp;
photographing the cell around her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.640
stuffing each freshly printed photo into her&nbsp;
pocket as the camera spat them out. Over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:42.000
span of forty-five minutes, she took a series&nbsp;
of pictures of the entire cell, until the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.680
in the instant camera had run out. Danielle knew&nbsp;
that she would probably only need one picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.880
but better to have more, just in case. Luckily,&nbsp;
none of her Foundation colleagues were around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.640
to tell her how insane what she was doing&nbsp;
was. They were all preoccupied, preparing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.240
site for its first official visit from the FBI.
This all took place after what was known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.920
Broken Masquerade had taken place. Essentially,&nbsp;
knowledge of the SCP Foundation and its research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.040
into the endless stream of anomalies it had&nbsp;
contained was all made public. People knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:21.760
truth now, and the Foundation found itself in the&nbsp;
one place it never wanted to be: the public eye.&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.480
The organization and its activities were under&nbsp;
the scrutiny of every journalist on the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.760
and once more information about the SCPs and the&nbsp;
anomalous world of the Foundation became known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:37.520
the world would surely change forever. Danielle&nbsp;
Kowalski knew all of this and told herself that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.360
everyone would need something for protection now.&nbsp;
She only hoped her photographs would be enough.&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.320
Fifteen months later, Doctor Kowalski&nbsp;
was interrogated in her apartment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.600
by one Agent O’Brien. He and a group of&nbsp;
fellow agents had barged into Danielle’s home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:07:00.560
demanding to know the location where a number of&nbsp;
SCP-610 specimens were being held. SCP-610 being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.920
of course, the infamous infection known as the&nbsp;
Flesh that Hates, a disease capable of turning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.920
human beings into horrible, deformed monstrosities&nbsp;
of flesh. Agent O’Brien, and presumably whoever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:17.360
he was working for, was intent on turning these&nbsp;
creatures into weapons, and the man had little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.400
patience for a former Foundation researcher who&nbsp;
wasn’t giving up the answers that he wanted.&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.520
Danielle tried to argue with the Agent, citing&nbsp;
the Berlin Accords Against Weaponized Anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.640
a motion proposed to the United States Senate&nbsp;
that would forbid the use of anomalous objects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.000
entities, and creatures as weapons for any&nbsp;
country. O’Brien wasn’t interested, he claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.040
that if the Kremlin in Russia had no intention&nbsp;
of ratifying such a law, then America wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.160
either. The Agent had his men grab Danielle by her&nbsp;
arm, while he grabbed her right middle finger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.040
began to push a thin knife under her fingernail.
The former member of the Foundation tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.200
stifle her scream, but couldn’t&nbsp;
stop herself as the pain struck.&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.760
As Agent O’Brien tortured Danielle, he told&nbsp;
her that the Russians had all the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:07.600
they would need about SCP-610 in order to&nbsp;
turn the Flesh into some kind of weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.000
As far as he was concerned, the United&nbsp;
States government needed access to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.040
SCP-610 specimens to know exactly what they&nbsp;
would be up against in the likely event that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.960
Russia used the Flesh that Hates against them.&nbsp;
And only a researcher for the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:29.360
the former senior researcher of the SCP-610&nbsp;
project, would know where to find those specimens.&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.000
“You don't think it's reasonable for the United&nbsp;
States government to want to understand what could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.800
be used against us?” O’Brien asked as Danielle’s&nbsp;
blood trickled out of her finger into his leather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:45.200
gloves. “Hell, I don't care what you think we're&nbsp;
going to do with it. I want those specimens.”&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.840
When the Agent finally let her go, Doctor&nbsp;
Kowalski tried to catch her breath, forcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.440
herself not to scream in pain again. She pointed&nbsp;
with her other, unharmed hand, to her bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.720
telling O’Brien to check the bottom left drawer.&nbsp;
She had placed a manilla envelope in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.080
containing the only thing Danielle had kept since&nbsp;
departing the SCP Foundation. Under O’Brien’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:11.360
order, his men ransacked the nearby room and were&nbsp;
quickly able to find what they were looking for.&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.800
The envelope was presented to Agent O’Brien, who&nbsp;
was no doubt expecting to find a file inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.320
something that detailed the exact location of&nbsp;
those specimens he was looking for. So, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:28.560
probably imagine the dissatisfied look on his face&nbsp;
when he opened the envelope to find… photographs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:35.200
A collection of instant photos, all taken in a&nbsp;
dark cell, with all of them depicting a tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:41.280
thin humanoid creature. A being with long,&nbsp;
dangling arms that almost reached the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:47.680
a thin, hairless body, opaque, pupil-less&nbsp;
eyes, and a wide, gaping mouth.&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.200
Furious to once again be denied&nbsp;
the information he was looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.680
O’Brien disregarded the pictures and burned&nbsp;
Doctor Kowalski’s neck with his cigarette.&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:00.240
In his anger, he said “I think your Foundation&nbsp;
sure thought they were powerful hiding in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:06.400
shadows. But I'd like to explain to you why you're&nbsp;
wrong.” The Agent then went on to describe his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.680
time as a member of the DEA in the late 1980’s,&nbsp;
and how he’d been involved in the hunting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:17.040
notorious cartel boss Pablo Escobar.
“I had a narco in custody… every bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.640
as uncooperative as you are now.” The man had&nbsp;
insisted that Escobar owned all of the Colombian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.000
law enforcement, but a younger O’Brien had argued&nbsp;
that, if that was the case and they possessed all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:34.240
this power, then why did Pablo Escobar’s men&nbsp;
hide their guns? Why smuggle drugs in secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:39.360
and bury their money where no one would find&nbsp;
it if they really had the control they claimed?&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.640
Much like Escobar’s cartel, the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation had always moved in the shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.920
a power that O’Brien believed to have really&nbsp;
belonged to the government. “Your anomalies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:58.640
belong to us. Your research belongs to us. Your&nbsp;
life belongs to us,” he told Doctor Kowalski.&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.200
Suddenly, one of the other agents noticed movement&nbsp;
outside the apartment. The second that Danielle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.880
saw a long arm phase through her wall, with grey&nbsp;
skin like that of a corpse, she snapped her eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.440
shut. O’Brien and his men all drew their guns&nbsp;
and opened fire on the monster that had walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.440
through the wall as if it wasn’t even there.
All the while, Danielle Kowalski kept her eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:25.520
shut. The sounds of screaming and snapping bones&nbsp;
and a sickening slurping noise filled the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:31.520
but she made sure not to look. Within a few&nbsp;
quick moments of panic and agonizing pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:36.160
it was over. The apartment was&nbsp;
quiet, apart from strange breathing;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.600
like a mix of a frightened&nbsp;
whimper and a death rattle.&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.080
“Run along now, 096,” Doctor Kowalski&nbsp;
told the Shy Guy. “There are worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.800
monsters in this world than you.”
Now go check out “SCP-096 Shy Guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:55.840
ESCAPE - Incident 096-1-A Containment Breach”&nbsp;
and “SCP-096 - Look at a Picture of Shy Guy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:01.360
Space? The Shy Guy Questions and Theories” for&nbsp;
more on everyone’s favorite homicidal introvert!

